$\n\\begin{aligned}{30}^{2}=30\\cdot 30=900\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443074 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443074 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75518" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443074 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443074 #edb/ref 1071 "${(\\frac{1}{8})}^{2}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443074 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "28839411-a8dd-5863-a44c-b7b61fd48e0a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443074 #edb/ref 1074 "To work out \n${(\\frac{1}{8})}^{2},$\n we multiply \n$\\frac{1}{8}$\n by itself:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{8})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}=\\frac{1}{64}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443075 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443075 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104534" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443075 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443075 #edb/ref 1071 "$(-3.2)^{2}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443075 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "28d7dd84-346e-5b73-9ead-af1c62abb54d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443075 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to calculate the product of $-3.2$ with itself:\n\n$$\n(-3.2)^{2}=(-3.2)\\cdot (-3.2)=(3.2)\\cdot (3.2)\n$$\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $32\\times 32=1\\,024.$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in the first factor ($3.2$) and $1$ decimal place in the second factor ($3.2$), so their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $1\\,024$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n10\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,{{24}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${3.2}^{2}=10.24.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443076 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443060 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443076 #edb/ref 1069 "q-185186" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443076 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443076 #edb/ref 1071 "Expressed as an improper fraction or mixed number, ${(-\\frac{9}{7})}^{2}=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443076 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "397f8e4c-8a02-58fc-a48a-6b5a482557f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443076 #edb/ref 1074 "To work out ${(-\\frac{9}{7})}^{2},$ we multiply $-\\frac{9}{7}$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(-\\frac{9}{7})}^{2} & = \\\\ (-\\frac{9}{7})\\cdot (-\\frac{9}{7}) & = \\\\ \\frac{9\\cdot 9}{7\\cdot 7} & = \\\\ \\frac{81}{49} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443077 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443077 #edb/ref 1069 "q-40858" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443077 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443077 #edb/ref 1071 "${4}^{2}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443077 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "44cc42c9-2404-5dbe-b1d3-8cf7bb1969eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443077 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute ${4}^{2},$ we multiply $4$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{2}=4\\cdot 4=16\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443078 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443078 #edb/ref 1069 "q-171165" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443078 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443078 #edb/ref 1071 "Expressed as a decimal in its simplest form, $(-1.4)^{2}=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443078 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "58bd0ac9-9ea6-5ce8-9fc3-fc8a73b17344" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443078 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to calculate the product of $(-1.4)$ with itself:\n\n$$\n(-1.4)^{2}=(-1.4)\\cdot (-1.4)=(1.4)\\cdot (1.4)\n$$\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{1}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $14\\times 14=196.$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in the first factor ($1.4$) and $1$ decimal place in the second factor ($1.4$), so their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $196$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,{{96}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(-1.4)^{2}=1.96\\,.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443079 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443079 #edb/ref 1069 "q-185190" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443079 #edb/